  "rules": [
    "Model forecasts exhibit a severe upward bias when recent history shows high volatility or mixed sentiment (e.g., Example 1 and 2), often predicting a smooth linear increase while the true outcome is highly volatile with sharp drops followed by spikes.",
    "When sentiment history contains low values (0.00) but recent price action is declining, the model fails to capture immediate downside risk, resulting in significant under-prediction of short-term lows.",
    "The model systematically overestimates the magnitude and smoothness of upward moves after a price spike; true outcomes often show delayed peaks or mean-reversion that the linear forecast misses entirely.",
    "In periods of high sentiment (0.20-0.40) combined with volatile history, the model's assumption of monotonic growth leads to large errors in the middle-to-late horizon where volatility reasserts itself.",
    "The forecast horizon is too long for short-term mean reversion; corrections should reduce the predicted price increase by 15-20% and introduce a higher variance term when sentiment history is inconsistent."
  ]
}
